name: scripts
boss: SIGEXIT
flavor: Whatever you started, I run last.
brief: Everything so far was a line you typed. A script is the same shell
brief: reading the lines instead of you -- which means it needs three things
brief: a prompt never does: arguments, structure, and a way to clean up.
brief:
brief: A function is structure. `name() { ... }` defines it, the bare name runs
brief: it. `local` keeps a variable inside it. `return` ends it with an exit
brief: status, the way `exit` ends the whole script.
brief:
brief: Arguments arrive as `$1 $2 $3`. `shift` drops `$1` and slides the rest down,
brief: which is how you walk a list you did not write. `getopts` walks option
brief: letters instead, one call per option.
brief:
brief: `trap` is the cleanup. `trap CMD EXIT` runs CMD however the shell ends --
brief: normally, by error, or killed -- so a temp file you made gets removed
brief: on the path you did not plan for.
brief:
brief: `exec` and `.` both act on the shell you are in rather than a child: `exec`
brief: replaces it or rewires its file descriptors for good, `.` runs a script
brief: here so what it sets survives. Neither is undoable mid-line.
---
q: {define a function f that echoes ok, then run it|write f so it echoes ok, then call it|f should `echo` ok -- define it, then run it}
a: f() { echo ok; }; f
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: the braces hold the body, and the bare name on its own calls it
---
setup: this function's v shouldn't stomp on a variable the caller already has
q: {define f to put 1 in v with `local` and `echo` v, then run f|with `local`, give f its own v of 1, `echo` v, then run f|f keeps its own v of 1 via `local` -- `echo` v, then run f}
a: f() { local v=1; echo $v; }; f
cmp: ref
ref: local
teach: local
hint: local keeps v inside f, so the caller never sees it change
---
q: {define f to `return` 3, run it, then `echo` the exit status|f should `return` 3 -- run it, then `echo` the status it left|make f `return` 3, call it, then `echo` what it left behind}
a: f() { return 3; }; f; echo $?
cmp: ref
ref: return
teach: return
hint: return ends the function with a status, the way exit ends a script
---
q: {`set` `--` a b c, drop the first with shift, then `echo` the new first|after `set` `--` a b c, shift once, then `echo` the first|`set` `--` a b c, shift the first away, then `echo` what heads the list}
a: set -- a b c; shift; echo $1
cmp: ref
ref: shift
teach: shift
hint: shift slides every parameter down one, which is how you walk a list
---
q: {`set` `--` a b c, `shift` by two, then `echo` what is now first|`set` `--` a b c, then `shift` two along and `echo` the first|two off the front of `set` `--` a b c with `shift`, then `echo` it}
a: set -- a b c; shift 2; echo $1
cmp: ref
ref: shift
teach: shift
hint: a count shifts that many at once
---
q: {`set` `--` `-a` x, read one option letter with `getopts` into o, `echo` o|`set` `--` `-a` x, then have `getopts` put one letter in o and `echo` o|after `set` `--` `-a` x, `getopts` one letter into o, then `echo` o}
a: set -- -a x; getopts a o; echo $o
cmp: ref
ref: getopts
teach: getopts
hint: the letters you declare are the ones it accepts, one call per option
---
q: {run @script@ in this shell with dot, so it is not a child|source @script@ with dot, so it runs here and not in a child|dot-run @script@ so what it sets survives in this shell}
a: . ./@script@
cmp: ref
ref: .
teach: .
hint: a dot-run script can change this shell, and a child could not
---
setup: a file needs to go back to empty without deleting it outright
q: {empty f with the null command, then count its bytes with `wc`|truncate f using the null command, then `wc` its byte count|blank f with the null command, then ask `wc` how many bytes}
a: : > f; wc -c < f
cmp: ref
ref: :
teach: :
hint: the null command does nothing and succeeds, so the redirect is the point
---
q: {`trap` EXIT to `echo` ``bye``, then `echo` ``work``|`trap` ``bye`` onto EXIT with `echo`, then `echo` ``work``|arrange for `echo` ``bye`` on EXIT with `trap`, then `echo` ``work``}
a: trap 'echo bye' EXIT; echo work
cmp: ref
ref: trap
teach: trap
hint: EXIT fires however the shell ends, which is where cleanup belongs
---
setup: every command from here on should send its errors where stdout already goes, without redirecting each one
q: {send errors to stdout with `exec`, then `ls` the missing ``nosuch``|point errors at stdout with `exec`, then `ls` ``nosuch``, which isn't there|rewire errors to stdout using `exec`, then `ls` the absent ``nosuch``}
a: exec 2>&1; ls nosuch
cmp: ref
ref: exec
teach: exec
hint: exec with only redirections rewires this shell instead of replacing it
---
setup: this script's only job left is to hand off to one program, not linger after it
q: {replace this shell with `echo` `done`, using `exec`|`exec` into `echo` `done`, so this shell becomes it|hand this shell off to `echo` `done` with `exec`}
a: exec echo done
cmp: ref
ref: exec
teach: exec
hint: with a command, exec becomes it -- nothing after the line ever runs
---
setup: the command wasn't written as one line -- it came together piece by piece
q: {put the text echo hi in c, then run what it holds with `eval`|c holds the text echo hi -- `eval` it to run what's in there|assign echo hi to c as text, then `eval` c to run it}
a: c="echo hi"; eval $c
cmp: ref
ref: eval
teach: eval
hint: eval expands the line a second time, then runs the result
---
setup: this value must never change again, even by an accidental later assignment
q: {pin r to 1 with `readonly`, then try to assign 2 to it|make r `readonly` at 1, then try assigning 2 anyway|`readonly` r as 1, then attempt 2 and see what happens}
a: readonly r=1; r=2
status: 2
cmp: ref
ref: readonly
teach: readonly
hint: a readonly name cannot be reassigned, and the attempt ends the shell
---
setup: you want the arithmetic done without wrapping it in $(( ))
q: {use `let` to work out 1 + 2 into x, then `echo` x|`let` x take 1 + 2, then `echo` x|work 1 + 2 into x with `let`, then `echo` x}
a: let x=1+2; echo $x
cmp: ref
ref: let
teach: let
hint: let does arithmetic without $(( )) around it
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: v=1; f() { local v=2; }; f; echo $v
a: 1
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: local keeps the assignment inside f -- the caller's v was never touched
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: set -- a b; shift 5; echo $#
a: 2
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: shift past the end of the list is a no-op, not an error -- $# is left exactly as it was
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: f() { return 1; }; f; echo after
a: after
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: return only ends the function -- the caller keeps running right after the call
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: f() { return 7; }; f; echo $?
a: 7
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: return's argument becomes the function's exit status, same as $? after any command
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: trap 'echo bye' EXIT
a: bye
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: EXIT fires when the shell ends for any reason, including just running off the last line
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: x='$y'; y=hi; eval echo $x
a: hi
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: the line expands $x to the literal text $y first -- eval then expands THAT, reaching y's value
